    whyItHappens: "Flagstaff, with its abundant sunshine and environmentally conscious population, became a prime target for solar companies. Many homeowners, eager to reduce their carbon footprint and save on rising Arizona Public Service (APS) utility bills, entered into solar contracts based on promises that often proved to be overly optimistic or outright misleading. The complexity of solar technology and the financial arrangements involved can make it difficult for consumers to fully understand what they are signing up for.\n\nArizona transitioned from traditional net metering to a 'resource comparison proxy' (RCP) rate, significantly lowering the credit for excess solar energy exported to the grid. These changes can significantly impact the financial viability of solar installations, leaving homeowners with systems that don't deliver the expected savings. Furthermore, the demographics of Flagstaff, with a mix of students, outdoor enthusiasts, and retirees, can lead to varying levels of understanding regarding solar technology and contract terms, making some more susceptible to high-pressure sales tactics.",
    stateLawExpanded: "Arizona provides robust consumer protections for homeowners entangled in problematic solar contracts through the Arizona Consumer Fraud Act (A.R.S. § 44-1521). This statute prohibits a wide range of deceptive acts or practices in the sale or advertisement of merchandise, including solar energy systems. This includes misrepresentations about energy savings, system performance, or contract terms. Consumers who have been harmed by such practices can seek actual damages, and in some cases, treble damages, as well as attorney's fees.\n\nThe Arizona Attorney General has a strong track record of pursuing consumer protection actions against companies engaging in deceptive practices, including those in the solar industry for misleading sales tactics. While specific public lawsuits against solar companies in Flagstaff may not be widely publicized, the AG's office has the authority to investigate complaints, issue cease and desist orders, and seek restitution for affected consumers across the state. This means if a solar company has engaged in widespread deceptive practices, the Attorney General could intervene on behalf of Flagstaff homeowners, providing a collective avenue for relief beyond individual lawsuits.",
    localFaq: [{"q":"My APS bill is still high after getting solar. Why?","a":"This is a common issue in Flagstaff, often due to systems underperforming, increased energy usage, or the drastic changes introduced by Arizona's transition to a 'resource comparison proxy' (RCP) rate, which significantly reduced the credit you receive for excess energy sent back to APS. Misleading sales pitches failing to account for these factors are also a frequent cause."},{"q":"Can I cancel my solar contract if the company lied to me?","a":"Potentially, yes. Arizona's Consumer Fraud Act (A.R.S. § 44-1521) protects you from deceptive practices. If the solar company made false promises about savings, system performance, or contract terms, you may have grounds to terminate the contract or seek damages. Legal counsel can assess your specific situation."},{"q":"What should I do if my solar system isn't working or needs constant repairs?","a":"First, check your contract for warranty information and contact the installer. If they are unresponsive or fail to resolve the issue, document everything. This could be a breach of contract or warranty, and depending on the severity and frequency of issues, it may provide grounds for legal action under Arizona consumer protection laws."},{"q":"How did Arizona's transition to RCP rates affect my solar contract?","a":"If you signed your contract after Arizona transitioned to RCP rates, it likely impacts the credit you receive from APS for surplus solar energy. This can dramatically extend the payback period and reduce expected savings. If your contract was based on pre-RCP assumptions without clear disclosure, you might have a claim for misrepresentation."}]
