    stateLawExpanded: "Michigan provides robust consumer protections for homeowners entangled in problematic solar contracts through the Michigan Consumer Protection Act (MCL § 445.901). This statute prohibits a wide range of unfair, unconscionable, or deceptive methods, acts, or practices in the conduct of trade or commerce. This includes misrepresentations about energy savings, system performance, or contract terms in the solar industry. Consumers who have been harmed by such practices can seek actual damages, and in some cases, attorney's fees.\n\nThe Michigan Attorney General has actively pursued cases against companies engaged in fraudulent business practices, including those impacting homeowners. While specific public lawsuits against solar companies in Flint may not be widely publicized, the AG's office has the authority to investigate complaints, issue cease and desist orders, and seek restitution for affected consumers across the state. This means if a solar company has engaged in widespread deceptive practices, the Attorney General could intervene on behalf of Flint homeowners, providing a collective avenue for relief beyond individual lawsuits.",
